ºw?ŸÚ·)zã?Žñt|±ãÑnJ8»Ûc£ÆËðR{"url":"/gcsl/working-with-files/file-and-process-effects/","fragment":{"page_number":0,"data":{"url":"/gcsl/working-with-files/file-and-process-effects/","content":"File and Process Effects. GCSL provides standard functions for files and subprocesses. Their effects use the host process's authority. Absolute paths and explicit command arguments make those effects easier to inspect, but don't restrict what a program can access. Locating files # Import fileroot to locate source directories and path to compose paths. fileroot.this is the directory of the file containing the expression; fileroot.workspace is the workspace root. fileroot.caller identifies the source directory of the running function's caller. path.join, path.normalize, path.dirname, and path.basename operate lexically. They don't resolve symbolic links or prove that a path stays inside a directory. Use them for path construction, not access control. File operations under fs require absolute paths. The following program writes and reads a temporary file, then removes its temporary directory when the run ends: Copyimport fs import path let directory = fs.temp_dir() let file = path.join(directory, \"message.txt\") fs.write(file, \"GCSL\") fs.read(file) Temporary directories last for the owning run, workspace, or REPL session. Normal cleanup also runs after an evaluation error. Killing the process can leave temporary directories behind. Atomic replacement by fs.write protects readers from partial contents; it does not preserve a replaced file's permissions or provide a durable transaction across several files. Running a process # exec accepts a list whose first element names the program. Later elements are passed as arguments without shell interpretation. It returns a record with the exit status and captured output. A nonzero exit status is data, not a GCSL runtime error. sh instead executes a string through /bin/sh and fails the GCSL run when the command fails. Don't insert untrusted text into that string. Prefer exec when values need to become arguments rather than shell code. exec_live and sh_live also copy child output to GCSL's standard error as lines arrive. They still capture output in memory. All these commands inherit the host environment and working directory, and give the child null standard input. A source-relative fileroot value does not change a command's working directory. Consult gcsl-stdlib(3) for exact function contracts and error behavior.","word_count":337,"filters":{},"meta":{"crumbs":"GCSL / Files and Workspaces","title":"File and Process Effects"},"anchors":[{"element":"h2","id":"locating-files","text":"Locating files #","location":39},{"element":"h2","id":"running-a-process","text":"Running a process #","location":200}]}},"word_data":{"relat":[{"position":318,"weight":12,"original_word":null}],"run":[{"position":75,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":130,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":154,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":162,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":200,"weight":144,"original_word":null},{"position":256,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"with":[{"position":227,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"nonzero":[{"position":235,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"transact":[{"position":196,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"them":[{"position":100,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"status":[{"position":230,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":237,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"fileroot":[{"position":43,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":53,"weight":12,"original_word":null},{"position":63,"weight":12,"original_word":null},{"position":68,"weight":12,"original_word":null},{"position":319,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"behind":[{"position":174,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"that":[{"position":92,"weight":24,"original_word":null},{"position":266,"weight":24,"original_word":null}],"shell":[{"position":221,"weight":24,"original_word"